# B2F golden transcript — fixture #1.
#
# PROVENANCE — read this before trusting a byte of it.
# This is a CONSTRUCTED transcript, assembled from the published ARSFI B2F grammar
# (winlink.org/B2F) and the FBB forward protocol (f6fbb.org/fbbdoc/docfwpro.htm). It is NOT a
# capture of a live CMS session, and it cannot settle any of the wire questions the programme
# spec calls undecidable (the ;PR: digest derivation, the LZHUF CRC16 coverage, the exact
# greeting line order). Its job is to pin THIS implementation's behaviour across chunk
# boundaries and to give the five negative controls something to perturb. The real oracle is
# fixture #2 — the byte stream captured at the first live CMS connect (spec §6 step 5) — and
# where the two ever disagree, fixture #2 wins and this file is corrected, never the reverse.
#
# Every byte here was produced OUTSIDE the code under test except one: the LZHUF image inside
# the binary record, which came from crates/tempo-core/src/winlink/lzhuf.rs (there is no other
# source for a Winlink-compressed body available offline). The ;PR: token, both checksums and
# all of the framing were computed by an independent implementation, so they are a genuine
# cross-check rather than a restatement of the engine.
#
# ENCODING
# A line beginning with '#' is a comment, and so is an empty line; neither is part of the
# transcript. Every other line is one record:
#
#     <direction> <hex>
#
# <direction> is '<' for a record the peer sent to Nexus (inbound) or '>' for a record Nexus
# is expected to send to the peer (outbound). <hex> is the record's bytes as lowercase
# hexadecimal with no separators — hex rather than text because FBB records carry SOH/STX/EOT,
# length bytes, checksum bytes and an LZHUF body, none of which survive being written as
# characters. The record split is for legibility only: the replay concatenates each direction,
# so how the engine batches its writes is not pinned by this file.
#
# PARAMETERS the replay must use for the outbound side to match
#     callsign  N0CALL
#     password  NEXUSTEST   <- a made-up string that is not a credential for anything; the
#                              ;PR: line below is what the wl2k-go derivation returns for it
#                              against the ;PQ: challenge in this transcript.
#
# < the CMS announces itself: FBB forwarding (F), B2 message format (B2)
#   [WL2K-5.0-B2FWIHJM$]<CR>
< 5b574c324b2d352e302d4232465749484a4d245d0d

# < the secure-login challenge
#   ;PQ: 41913235<CR>
< 3b50513a2034313931333233350d

# > the forward-to list: this station's own callsign
#   ;FW: N0CALL<CR>
> 3b46573a204e3043414c4c0d

# > our SID
#   [Nexus-1.0-B2FHM$]<CR>
> 5b4e657875732d312e302d423246484d245d0d

# > the challenge answered (see the header note on the password)
#   ;PR: 74706169<CR>
> 3b50523a2037343730363136390d

# > the identity comment line; the target is the peer SID's product field
#   ; WL2K DE N0CALL<CR>
> 3b20574c324b204445204e3043414c4c0d

# > nothing to propose; over to you
#   FF<CR>
> 46460d

# < one message offered: 598 bytes uncompressed, 365 compressed
#   FC EM ABCDEFGHIJKL 598 365 0<CR>
< 464320454d204142434445464748494a4b4c203539382033363520300d

# < the block checksum: two's complement of the sum of the FC line's bytes, CR included
#   F> 76<CR>
< 463e2037360d

# > one answer, in proposal order: send it
#   FS +<CR>
> 4653202b0d

# < SOH header, 2 STX data blocks (0x00 length = 256), EOT + checksum
#   <SOH><0F>ABCDEFGHIJKL<00>0<00><STX><00>sZV<STX><00><00><EC><FD>~<1C>mg7<9D><CF><E8>tz]><A7> ... (388 bytes)
< 

# < the transfer is done and the peer has nothing more
#   FF<CR>
< 46460d

# > we have nothing either: end the session
#   FQ<CR>
> 46510d
